      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Setup, refinement strength and limits",
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Upscale and refine with the same references\n\n1. Select an already-generated image in Load Image. Use its original scene prompt in the positive text node.\n2. Select the SAME person's saved bundle for this model family, and the matching VAE, model and encoder. Do not use a Flux.2 bundle with Flux 3; bake a separate bundle from the source photos for that family.\n3. Start with 1.69 megapixels: approximately 1536x1152 for a 1024x768 source. Aspect ratio is retained and dimensions round to a 16-pixel grid. This is a target pixel area, so smaller inputs get enlarged more. Keep the target larger than your input.\n4. Denoise starts at 0.20. Lower it to preserve the generated face/composition more closely; raise cautiously toward 0.30 for stronger redraw. RefMod references remain active throughout the refinement. The sampler receives the VAE-encoded upscaled IMAGE, not an empty latent.\n\nLanczos provides the initial enlargement without a required external upscaler. The diffusion pass synthesizes detail; it does not recover guaranteed original skin, beard or tattoo detail. Compare identity as well as texture. If the face occupies only a small part of a wide shot, a targeted face-crop refinement may be more effective than whole-image enlargement.\n\nFlux.2 Klein Base 4B uses Qwen3-4B, CFG 5 and a size-aware Flux2Scheduler. The 100-step schedule feeds SplitSigmasDenoise at 0.20; its LOW sigma output keeps the last 20 refinement steps. If changing this fraction, the executed step count changes too. Output width/height are wired from the resized image automatically."
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "text": "# Upscale and refine with the same references\n\n1. Select an already-generated image in Load Image. Use its original scene prompt in the positive text node.\n2. Select the SAME person's saved bundle for this model family, and the matching VAE, model and encoder. Do not use a Flux.2 bundle with Flux 3; bake a separate bundle from the source photos for that family.\n3. Start with 1.69 megapixels: approximately 1536x1152 for a 1024x768 source. Aspect ratio is retained and dimensions round to a 16-pixel grid. This is a target pixel area, so smaller inputs get enlarged more. Keep the target larger than your input.\n4. Denoise starts at 0.20. Lower it to preserve the generated face/composition more closely; raise cautiously toward 0.30 for stronger redraw. RefMod references remain active throughout the refinement. The sampler receives the VAE-encoded upscaled IMAGE, not an empty latent.\n\nLanczos provides the initial enlargement without a required external upscaler. The diffusion pass synthesizes detail; it does not recover guaranteed original skin, beard or tattoo detail. Compare identity as well as texture. If the face occupies only a small part of a wide shot, a targeted face-crop refinement may be more effective than whole-image enlargement.\n\nFlux.2 Klein Base 4B uses Qwen3-4B, CFG 5 and a size-aware Flux2Scheduler. The 100-step schedule feeds SplitSigmasDenoise at 0.20; its LOW sigma output keeps the last 20 refinement steps. If changing this fraction, the executed step count changes too. Output width/height are wired from the resized image automatically."
      }
    },
